# Page history

## Previous versions from git (`history({ source: "git" })`, `versions.ts`)

- The version pages are page artifacts (`pages/<path below the mount>.svelte`) and routes added to `ctx.index.routes.all` in a normal-tier `emitArtifacts` hook, before `core:emit-artifacts` (`enforce: "post"`) writes both. Never `ProcessedFile`s: those would reach search, the explorer, the graph, tags, backlinks, feeds and sitemaps. The theme's note route finds the artifact by path and, with no index entry, renders it bare in the shell.
- Check every address against `routes.all`, note slugs and the theme's routes (`matchThemeRoute`, which must find a route without a `component`) and fail the build naming the note: a silent collision serves the wrong page.
- A version compiles with the build's own compiler steps (`ctx.compiler`), plus only the steps its content alone registers: steps come from conditional detection over every note, embed targets included, so a fresh collection loses math a version only embeds, and appending the stages' fresh registrations to the build's applies each step twice.
- A version compiles through `compileNoteVersion` (`@svartz/plugins`), the vault's own stages and compiler, never `renderRevision`: that is the live mode's preview renderer, and it lacks math, highlighting and every step plugins add. The page component is the note's Svelte markup inside the page's own HTML, braces escaped, so it takes `contentComponents` like a note.
- Links are relative to the page: the note's relative URLs get `../../` (from `<note>versions/<sha>/`), since only SvelteKit knows the deployment base. A relative URL naming nothing in `routes.all` or the published files shows unresolved: a dead link in an old version can't be fixed and would fail the prerender crawl.
- Attachments a shown version references are published with it: from the working tree (discovery's list, kept in `ctx.meta` by the plugin's `filterUnpublished` hook before core drops unreferenced files), else as bytes from git at that version's commit, by blob at `<note>versions/files/<blob>/<name>` with the version's links moved there (`compileNoteVersion`'s `moved`). Never publish a deleted file by its vault path: versions held different bytes at it. `keepsAttachment` and the build's kept set (protected-only files included) decide; a hidden version's attachments are never read.
- A version shows only when `publishesNote` (from `@svartz/plugins`, the build's own rule) holds at the path it had then and it has no `password_group`. Diff consecutive *shown* versions; never read past a hidden one into a page. A summary follows the live rule below, plus the note's own shown paths so a rename's summary shows.
- When no note has two versions to show, add no artifact, route or browser resource: the build must stay byte-identical (the CLI e2e compares it).
- Git calls go through `git.ts` with `-z`/NUL parsing; `%aN` only, never an email format.
- Compiled versions are cached (`cache.ts`) in `<outDir>/../cache/versions/`. Anything a version's compile reads goes in its key; a stale page is worse than a slow build. Leave out only the note's own current file, which the version replaces in its compile, so a commit to the note recompiles just its new version. Decide visibility before `cache.get`, and write only versions that compiled cleanly. `emitVersions` prunes in its `finally` after a walk that saw every note, so the cache holds only versions that show; a failed build prunes nothing, since it never reached what it would have reused, and a hidden version's entry is never served. Plugin `options` and the contents of plugin `reads` files are in the key: a value a hook only closes over isn't.
- A version reused from the cache must give the build what compiling it would have: its markup, its attachments (snapshots by key and blob), and its browser resources. Anything else `compileNoteVersion` does to the build has to be cached too, or a warm build differs from a cold one (the plugin-history and CLI tests compare them).

## Live history from the mirror

- History reads commits only through `@svartz/plugin-submissions/commits`: never the `submissions_*` tables with its own SQL, never GitHub with credentials of its own. What it needs from the mirror or GitHub goes into that file.
- `commitSource(ctx)` reads the vault folder submissions' `emitArtifacts` recorded, so history's `emitArtifacts` runs with `enforce: "post"`.
- Every GitHub request a reader triggers goes through `get()` in `commits.ts`: it spends the reader's budget (`MAX_GITHUB_READS`) and checks the shared pause first, and pauses the poll too when GitHub says to wait or the allowance reaches the reserve. A request that bypasses it can spend the requests kept for saves.
- A revision sha reaches GitHub only after `onBranch` (mirror, or `compare/<sha>...<branch>` saying the branch holds it): GitHub serves a fork's commits through the parent repository.
- Order changes by ancestry, never by commit time or sha: a page's history and `previous()` come from GitHub's list for the file, since push deliveries don't name parents and times tie or are backdated.
- A commit names an account only when `submissions_edits` records it: a direct commit by its sha, a submission by its pull request's merge commit. Never from git metadata (author, committer, `Svartz-Account` trailer): whoever makes a commit writes all of it.
- Show only what the build published unprotected: history pages for published, unlocked `.md` notes; a summary only when every vault file of the commit is known (`allFiles`: GitHub pages a commit's files past 300, and a `Link` to a next page means a part) and published; no content of a revision whose frontmatter made it a draft, private or password-protected.
- Commit text is untrusted: escape it, and render revisions through `renderRevision` (`@svartz/plugin-submissions/render`, shared with the review panel), which applies the build's sanitizing rules (`@svartz/plugins/content`).
- `CommitReader.headFile` reads a pull request's head, which isn't on the branch, and skips `onBranch`: only for a SHA GitHub itself just named as this repository's pull request head, never one from a URL. `file` keeps the `onBranch` check because both share one cache.
- `MADE_BY_SITE` (whose commit is it) and `MADE_BY_ACCOUNT` (which commits are this account's) in `commits.ts` must credit the same saves: the earliest save per commit by `(saved_at, id)`, so a follow-up to someone's pull request never takes its merge. Change them together.
- An account's next reputation threshold shows only to the account itself (`readAccount(...).id === findStanding(...).id`): thresholds name roles, which only admins otherwise see.
- A user page's section is server-rendered HTML that the browser script puts after `[data-svartz-note]` (`note.after()`), as annotations does. Never render it into the prerendered note.
- Seed many saves in tests with `recordCommits` (submissions' `recordEdit` on the real D1) and push commits with `siteMessage`, the form the site writes: a commit-kind save's summary is the note between its title and trailers, empty for a one-line message.
- Revert writes only through `@svartz/plugin-submissions/restore` (`restorer`), which commits with `commitSubmission` as the account and records the save with `earns: false`. Never a GitHub client of history's own, and never a pull request when the branch refuses: answer `protected`.
- The revert form carries the head the moderator saw; `revertRoute` compares it with GitHub's list for the file (`reader.history(path, 0)`, which also bounds a revert to the latest 100 changes) and commits over that head's blob. Check permission on the page and, through `restorer.claims` and `canEach`, on every page the restored frontmatter claims, before any write.
- `restore()` takes `undoes` (GitHub's list for the file, newer than the restored revision), records them with the save, then takes back what their saves earned: the mirror may not know yet that one of them landed.
- Order: GitHub reads and every refusal, then `restore()` with the audit entry in `beforeMove` (the commit exists, the branch hasn't moved; with the hook `commitSubmission` doesn't retry a moved branch, so one entry names the one commit). Inside it, the record and the take-back each log their failure: a landed commit is never answered as failed, and submissions' `credit()` retries a take-back that failed (see `reputation.mdc`). The route itself never touches reputation.
- One code path for reverting: the history list links rows to the diff page (`?from=<R>&to=<head>#sv-history-revert`), and moderation's Reports panel links a reported commit's diff; only the diff page renders the form.
